lesson: 181
title: Sure of several of the same colour
level: primary-3
stars: 2
explain: To be sure of something, think of the unluckiest draw: it takes as many as it can without the wish coming true.
explain: With three colours, the unlucky hand holds one less than wanted of every colour; the very next one makes the wish come true.
explain: To be sure of 4 socks of one colour from red, blue and white socks: 3 + 3 + 3 = 9 can still fail, so 10 are sure.
vars: a=name r=5..15 b=5..15 w=5..15 k=2..5
answer: 3 * (k - 1) + 1
exercise: A drawer holds {r} red, {b} blue and {w} white socks. In the dark, what is the least number of socks {a} must take out to be sure of {k} socks of the same colour?
exercise: A bag has {r} red, {b} green and {w} yellow marbles. What is the smallest number of marbles {a} must pull out without looking to be sure that {k} of them are the same colour?
exercise: In a box there are {r} red, {b} blue and {w} black pens. How many pens must {a} take with closed eyes to be certain of getting {k} pens of one colour?
exercise: A jar holds {r} orange, {b} purple and {w} pink candies. {a} takes candies without looking. What is the least number {a.he} must take to be sure of {k} candies of the same colour?
exercise: A basket holds {r} white, {b} grey and {w} brown balls of wool. How many balls must {a} pick blindly to be sure that {k} of them have the same colour?
test: {a} wants {k} gloves of one colour. The cupboard is dark, and it holds {r} black, {b} white and {w} red gloves. How few gloves can {a.he} grab and still be certain?
test: Without looking, {a} draws beads from a bag of {r} blue, {b} green and {w} red beads. At least how many draws guarantee {k} beads of a single colour?
test: To be certain of {k} same-coloured balloons, how many balloons must be taken blindly from a sack of {r} yellow, {b} red and {w} blue ones?
exercise: What is the fewest socks {a} must take from a dark drawer of {r} red, {b} blue and {w} white socks to be sure of {k} of one colour?
exercise: A sweet jar in a shop in Leeds holds {r} red, {b} green and {w} yellow gumdrops. Without looking, how many must {a} take out to be certain of {k} gumdrops of the same colour?
exercise: Without looking, {a} picks crayons from a box of {r} red, {b} blue and {w} green crayons. At least how many must {a.he} pick to be sure of {k} crayons of one colour?
exercise: {r} red, {b} blue and {w} white marbles are in a bag. Draw without looking. How many draws make {k} of one colour certain?
exercise: A box in a classroom in Toronto has {r} red, {b} yellow and {w} blue counters. What is the least number of counters {a} must take blindfolded to be sure that {k} of them match in colour?
exercise: In a cupboard in Sydney there are {r} green, {b} pink and {w} blue towels. With the light off, how many towels must {a} take to be certain of {k} towels of the same colour?
exercise: {a} has a bag of {r} red, {b} white and {w} black buttons. At least how many buttons must {a.he} take out without looking to be sure that {k} have the same colour?
exercise: A box of fruit sweets in Dublin has {r} lemon, {b} lime and {w} orange sweets, all wrapped alike. How many sweets must {a} take to be sure of {k} sweets of one flavour?
test: How few pencils must be taken blindly from a pencil case with {r} red, {b} blue and {w} green pencils to guarantee {k} pencils of one colour?
test: {a} is packing in a dark tent in New Zealand. A bag holds {r} red, {b} yellow and {w} blue tent pegs. How many pegs must {a.he} grab to be sure that {k} of them share a colour?
test: A gumball machine in Ohio has {r} red, {b} green and {w} white gumballs, and each coin gives a gumball at random. How many coins must {a} spend to be certain of {k} gumballs of the same colour?
test: Blindfolded, {a} takes T-shirts from a pile of {r} black, {b} grey and {w} white ones. What is the smallest number that makes sure {k} T-shirts are of the same colour?
test: {k} ribbons of one colour are wanted. A box in a craft shop in Melbourne has {r} red, {b} gold and {w} silver ribbons, and they are taken without looking. How many must be taken to be certain?
test: A hat holds {r} red, {b} blue and {w} green tickets. At the least, how many tickets must {a} draw blind so that some colour surely appears {k} times?
